{
  // PL-04 / item 4: the root tsconfig.json's "include" is ["src"] only (apps/web's TypeScript
  // source) — `npx tsc --noEmit` from the repo root has never type-checked anything under scripts/,
  // so every "tsc clean" this control-plane tooling has reported was vacuous for it: a call to a
  // function that doesn't exist, a typo'd property, or a wrong argument count in any script here
  // would only ever be caught by a test actually exercising that code path, never by the type-check
  // step CLAUDE.md's own Verification order names first.
  //
  // This is the cheap option the ticket names: `checkJs` over the existing plain-JS `.mjs` files, no
  // new build step, no source rewrite. Not extending the root tsconfig.json on purpose — that config
  // is shaped for apps/web's browser/React code (`jsx`, the DOM lib, `useDefineForClassFields`), none
  // of which applies to a Node CLI script, and its `include`/`exclude` are unrelated to this
  // directory. `strict`/`noImplicitAny` are deliberately NOT enabled here (see README-less rationale
  // inline below): these files were written with no type annotations and only prose JSDoc, so turning
  // strict mode on today would surface a large batch of implicit-`any`-shaped findings unrelated to
  // any real defect, drowning out the signal this ticket actually wants — a wrong argument count, an
  // unresolvable import, a reference to a property that was renamed in one call site but not another.
  // `checkJs` alone already catches all of those. Tightening further (JSDoc `@param`/`@returns` types
  // on exported functions, then `strict: true`) is real future work, not done here — see this
  // ticket's own "judge how far to go" instruction.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  // Scoped to the SDD pipeline control plane specifically — the tooling planner/red/dev/tester/
  // review/security-checker, the ledger, and CI itself actually depend on for a correct verdict —
  // not literally every ".mjs" under scripts/. `scripts/` also holds four secret-scanning utilities
  // (check-secret-access.mjs(+.test.mjs), check-org-secret-access.mjs(+.test.mjs)) that are a
  // different subsystem entirely (a pre-install secrets guard — see ci.yml's separate "Secret-access
  // boundary" step) and own no pipeline-tooling behavior — that "different subsystem" argument is
  // the actual reason these four stay excluded, on its own, independent of whether any of them
  // happens to be clean.
  //
  // PL-15: this comment previously named nine excluded scripts — the four above, plus five
  // marketing-site rendering/QA utilities (the OG image renderer, the hero-animation renderer, the
  // smoke test, the waitlist checker, and the filetree exporter). Those five moved out of scripts/
  // entirely, into the new apps/site/scripts/ workspace-owned directory; they are no longer present
  // here at all, let alone excluded from this config. Only the four secret-scanning scripts remain.
  //
  // CORRECTED (PL-04 review pass 1 / W4): this comment previously also claimed all nine "carry
  // pre-existing, unrelated checkJs findings of their own," offered as a second, independent reason to
  // exclude them. That was only true of five — the five marketing-site scripts named above, none of
  // which are relevant to this config any longer (PL-15). The four secret-scanning scripts that
  // remain — check-secret-access.mjs, check-secret-access.test.mjs, check-org-secret-access.mjs,
  // check-org-secret-access.test.mjs — are clean today and could be folded into `include` at zero
  // cost; they stay excluded anyway, because the "different subsystem" reasoning above already
  // justifies it on its own and this config's job is to gate the pipeline control plane, not to
  // accumulate every clean script in the directory. Extending this config's scope to cover any of
  // the four is real, separate future work, not assumed done here.
  //
  // Every file listed below DOES type-check clean as of this round (verified: `npx tsc --noEmit -p
  // scripts/tsconfig.json` exits 0) — two real findings surfaced along the way and were fixed, not
  // suppressed: a stray 4th argument to `assert.equal` in check-red-integrity.test.mjs (silently
  // discarded at runtime, never a test failure, but genuinely dead code) and this file's own new
  // `assertKnownFlags` helper needing an explicit JSDoc parameter type (an inference gap the helper's
  // own multiple call sites exposed, not a defect in the flags logic itself).
  "include": [
    "build-ledger-index.mjs",
    "build-ledger-index.test.mjs",
    "check-bounds.mjs",
    "check-bounds.test.mjs",
    "check-lite-eligible.mjs",
    "check-lite-eligible.test.mjs",
    "check-red-integrity.mjs",
    "check-red-integrity.test.mjs",
    "check-stamp-freshness.mjs",
    "check-stamp-freshness.test.mjs",
    "generate-agent-shims.mjs",
    "generate-agent-shims.test.mjs",
    "guard-checker-readonly.mjs",
    "guard-checker-readonly.test.mjs",
    "ledger-append.mjs",
    "ledger-append.test.mjs",
    "pipeline-contracts.mjs",
    "pipeline-contracts.test.mjs",
    "sdd-completeness.test.mjs",
    "validate-security-audit-agents.mjs",
    "validate-security-audit-agents.test.mjs",
    "validate-security-audit-run.mjs",
    "validate-security-audit-run.test.mjs",
    "write-stamp.mjs",
    "write-stamp.test.mjs"
  ]
}
